  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "simplifyExpression",
    "file": "skia/src/sksl/SkSLCompiler.cpp",
    "description": "Compiler::simplifyExpression performs constant-propagation replacement in place (lines 696-710) and continues scanning when try_replace_expression reports that the CFG was updated cleanly. This is unsafe because DefinitionMap values are non-owning pointers to unique_ptr<Expression> slots inside the current AST (for example, addDefinitions records &BinaryExpression::right() for `x = 8`). Replacing an enclosing expression with a cloned simplification destroys the original subtree and poisons those slots, but the current DefinitionMap is not invalidated or recomputed. A later variable reference calls DefinitionMap::getKnownDefinition and dereferences the stale slot, producing the use-after-poison.",
    "conditions": "An optimized SkSL function must establish a known constant definition inside an expression subtree, then simplify an enclosing expression by cloning/replacing that subtree without forcing a CFG/data-flow rescan, and subsequently read the same variable in the current block. In the PoC `void n(){int x;(x*=1,(x=8).s0,x);}`, `x*=1` simplifies to a side-effect-free `x`; comma folding then replaces the enclosing comma expression with a clone of its right side containing `x=8`. The definition map still points at the RHS unique_ptr slot in the destroyed original `x=8` node. Constant propagation of the trailing `x` dereferences that poisoned pointer in getKnownDefinition."
  },
